        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[An hour of audio transcribed in about 13s on MacBook Air (281 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://eg2.haku.sh/</link>
            <guid>50069971</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:27:14 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://eg2.haku.sh/">https://eg2.haku.sh/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069971">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
  
  <p>Google's EmbeddingGemma 2 is an embedding model. We turned it into a speech-to-text model:
    <em>an hour of audio in about 13 seconds on a base MacBook Air</em>, entirely on device.</p>

  <p><a href="https://eg2.haku.sh/download">Download for Mac (free)</a>
    <span>v1.6 · macOS 14+ · Apple Silicon<br>models download on first launch (715 MB)</span>
  </p>

  <video src="https://eg2.haku.sh/film.mp4" poster="https://eg2.haku.sh/poster.jpg" controls="" playsinline="" preload="metadata"></video>

  <h2>Can an embedding model transcribe speech?</h2>
  <p>EmbeddingGemma 2 is a multimodal embedding model: text, images, audio and video in one shared space.
    Say a word out loud and its audio lands close to the written word.</p>
  <p>So we tried reading words straight out of that space.</p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>EmbeddingGemma 2's audio encoder stays exactly as it is.</li>
    <li>A small reader moves each short slice of audio into the model's text space.</li>
    <li>The closest of ~229,000 English words wins, and the slices become a transcript.</li>
    <li>A tiny extra model adds punctuation and capitals.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Only the reader is trained: under two hours on 8×H100, on openly licensed English speech.
    The base model was never trained for speech-to-text.</p>

  <h2>How fast?</h2>
  <div>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Mac</th><th>Speed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Embedding Gemma Transcribe</td><td>Neural Engine</td><td>base M3 MacBook Air, 8 GB</td>
          <td>288×<small>1 hour in 12.6 s</small></td></tr>
        <tr><td>IBM Granite Speech 5.0 TurboCTC</td><td>GPU (MLX)</td><td>base M3 MacBook Air, 8 GB</td>
          <td>~150×<small>1 hour in 25.7 s</small></td></tr>
        <tr><td>NVIDIA Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (FluidAudio)</td><td>Neural Engine</td><td>M4 Pro</td><td>156×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>NVIDIA Parakeet TDT 0.6B v3 (FluidAudio)</td><td>Neural Engine</td><td>M5 Pro</td><td>335×</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phonon-2, a Parakeet v3 fine-tune (FluidAudio)</td><td>Neural Engine</td><td>M5 Pro</td><td>480×</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>× real time: seconds of audio per second of processing. Rows 1–2: our tests on the same base M3 MacBook Air.
    Rows 3–5: <a href="https://github.com/FluidInference/FluidAudio/blob/main/Documentation/Benchmarks.md">FluidAudio's published benchmarks</a>
    on larger, newer Macs and different audio. A rough guide, not a like-for-like ranking.</p>

  <h2>Where it stands</h2>
  <p>Its word error rate is about 5% right now, against 2–3% for leading speech models. We believe a better-trained
    reader can close that gap; that's the next iteration.</p>

  <h2>Use it</h2>
  <p>Drop in an audio file, or hold Right Option, speak and release to dictate into any app. It runs on the Mac's
    Neural Engine; your audio never leaves the machine.</p>
  

  
</div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Character.ai Files for Bankruptcy (139 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1009802/character-ai-files-for-bankruptcy-disney</link>
            <guid>50069942</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 11:24:08 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1009802/character-ai-files-for-bankruptcy-disney">https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1009802/character-ai-files-for-bankruptcy-disney</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50069942">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="zephr-anchor"><p>Character.AI, one of the first breakout startups of the generative AI boom, has filed for bankruptcy and is selling some of its assets to Disney. The company, which builds various online bots for users to chat or roleplay with, has faced mounting scrutiny and <a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/858102/characterai-google-teen-suicide-settlement">lawsuits</a> over the <a href="https://www.theverge.com/report/841610/ai-chatbot-suicide-safety-failure">safety</a> of its <a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1009463/character-ai-chats-encouraging-self-harm-detailed-in-unredacted-kentucky-lawsuit">chatbots</a>, but has continued <a href="https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/951461/character-ai-is-adding-new-tools-for-chatbot-creators">releasing</a> updates and products in recent months.</p><p>In a <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20261012656385/en/Character.ai-Initiates-Voluntary-Chapter-11-Proceeding-to-Facilitate-Value-Maximizing-Sale-Process">press release</a> and <a href="https://blog.character.ai/an-update-on-character-ai/">blog post</a> published Monday, Character said it had filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy protection in the US to facilitate the sale of the company and its assets. “Over the past year, we’ve taken meaningful steps to sustain and improve our business amid significant legal, regulatory, and operational headwinds,” the company said.</p><p>In its bankruptcy <a href="https://cases.stretto.com/public/x936/14939/PLEADINGS/1493910122680000000088.pdf">filing</a>, Character listed estimated liabilities between $50 million and $100 million. As part of the proposed sale, Disney would serve as the lead bidder for Character.AI’s assets, setting a baseline for competing offers. Disney would pay $20 million for certain undisclosed assets, and provide transition support “using former Company employees it has hired.” It would also receive a nonexclusive license to unspecified intellectual property.</p><p>Disney’s involvement comes shortly after the entertainment behemoth <a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/997555/karandeep-anand-disney-character-ai">hired</a> Character.AI’s former CEO Karandeep Anand as chief technology officer, part of its growing push into AI. Anand’s tenure as Character CEO lasted just over a year and during that time he <a href="https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/962897/character-ai-series-microdrama-vertical-video">oversaw the company’s move into microdramas</a>. Disney has <a href="https://www.theverge.com/news/789451/its-curtains-for-ai-elsa-spider-man-and-darth-vader">previously complained</a> that Character was damaging its brand and exploiting its intellectual property by allowing unauthorized chatbots of its characters, including sending cease and desist letters.</p><p>Along with Anand, Disney also <a href="https://variety.com/2026/biz/news/disney-cto-karandeep-anand-character-ai-1236866528/">said</a> it expected “a number of Character.AI’s technical team” to join the company. The move resembles a kind of “acquihire” strategy that has become increasingly common in Silicon Valley as a means of hoovering up startups’ talent that sometimes leaves the original company struggling to survive. Character.AI has survived it once already — Google spent over $2 billion to <a href="https://www.theverge.com/2024/8/2/24212348/google-hires-character-ai-noam-shazeer">hire away</a> much of the company’s talent, including co-founders Noam Shazeer and Daniel De Freitas, in 2024.</p><p>Character said it expects the sale process to be completed by late November or early December and plans to continue operating its services, including paid subscriptions, in the meantime. However, the company warned that “the platform will be wound down” if a suitable bid does not emerge. “As we navigate this process, maintaining a safe and engaging experience for our community remains a top priority,” it said.</p><div><p><span><strong>Follow topics and authors</strong> from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates.</span></p><ul><li id="follow-author-article_footer-dmcyOmF1dGhvclByb2ZpbGU6NzY5ODkx"><span aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true" role="button" tabindex="0"><span><span><svg width="9" height="9" viewBox="0 0 9 9" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Follow"><path d="M5 0H4V4H0V5H4V9H5V5H9V4H5V0Z"></path></svg></span><span>Robert Hart</span></span></span></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li></ul></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
