            <title><![CDATA[Hittite royal archive found in Turkey (260 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/77107</link>
            <guid>50067414</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 06:02:51 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/77107">https://www.thehistoryblog.com/archives/77107</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50067414">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><article id="post-77107">
	<!-- .entry-header -->

	<div>
		<p><a href="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img title="Cuneiform tablets from the Hittite royal archive at Samuha. Photo courtesy Ministry of Culture and Tourism." src="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x336.jpg" alt="" width="350" height="274" srcset="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x336.jpg 430w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-200x156.jpg 200w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-150x117.jpg 150w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-768x601.jpg 768w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-1536x1201.jpg 1536w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-2048x1602.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 350px) 100vw, 350px"></a>Archaeologists have uncovered a <a href="https://www.dailysabah.com/life/hittite-royal-archive-with-184-tablets-unearthed-in-central-turkiye/news" target="_blank" rel="noopener">royal archive of cuneiform tablets</a> in the ancient Hittite city of Samuha, modern-day Sivas, central Turkey. This year’s excavation unearthed 131 cuneiform tablets and fragments which record an epidemic of fever, military logistics, religious practices and events of royal daily life 3,400 years ago. Last year’s excavation uncovered 53 cuneiform tablets and fragments, so this season’s discoveries more than triple the number of texts. It is now the largest, most comprehensive archive of Hittite-language documents found in the 21st century.</p>
<p>The records date to the reign of King Tudhaliya II, and are written in Hittite and Hurrian.&nbsp;One text tells of a fever epidemic that struck the infant daughter of the king. She was still a nursing baby when she became ill. She never recovered from the fever and her health declined precipitously until she passed away. The cuneiform tablet records the funerary ritual that followed.</p>
<p><a href="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" title="Cuneiform tablets from the Hittite royal archive at Samuha. Photo courtesy Ministry of Culture and Tourism." src="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x287.jpg" alt="" width="350" height="234" srcset="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x287.jpg 430w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-150x100.jpg 150w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-768x513.jpg 768w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-1536x1025.jpg 1536w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-2.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-2048x1367.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 350px) 100vw, 350px"></a>The health of the princes and princesses, not just babies but even up to adulthood, was an important focus of the records. The children of high-ranking imperial officials were given the same attention. Because illness was considered an expression of divine will, religious leaders performed many rituals to keep an eye on the physical health of the children of kings and government officials.</p>
<p>Another notable document is a letter from Hittite generals in western Anatolia appealing to the king for reinforcements in men, horses and chariots to fend off enemy forces. The generals’ warnings are dire. They plead that without an infusion of strength, the army would be destroyed by the enemy. They have the temerity to disclaim all responsibility for any upcoming failures if the reinforcements don’t come.</p>
<p><a href="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" title="Cuneiform tablets from the Hittite royal archive at Samuha. Photo courtesy Ministry of Culture and Tourism." src="http://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x287.jpg" alt="" width="350" height="234" srcset="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-430x287.jpg 430w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-200x133.jpg 200w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-150x100.jpg 150w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-768x513.jpg 768w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-1536x1025.jpg 1536w, https://www.thehistoryblog.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Cuneiform-tablets-from-the-Hittite-royal-archive-at-Kayalipinar-3.-Photo-courtesy-Ministry-of-Culture-and-Tourism.-2048x1367.jpg 2048w" sizes="(max-width: 350px) 100vw, 350px"></a>It’s a rare glimpse into the realities of military logistics with armies long distances from the king and court. The text also confirms that the king was at Samuha when the letter arrived, underscoring the city’s political importance. It was a royal stronghold for King Tudhaliya during times of military and political upheaval. His successor Suppiluliuma I used it as a launching pad for military campaigns that consolidated and expanded the territory of the Hittite Empire.</p>
<p>Samuha was also a major center of religious administration. Its patron deity was the goddess Sauska, goddess of love and war who was the Hurrian version of Ishtar and the most important goddess in the Hurrian pantheon. The association with the goddess was central to the city’s identity and was reflected in its religious ceremonies.</p>
<blockquote><p>One tablet records the journeys of bird diviners who left Samuha and traveled through different regions. Their routes, the cities where they stayed and the birds they observed were recorded in a format resembling a diary.&nbsp;The documents also provide previously unknown information about how bird omens were observed, evaluated and interpreted by the Hittites.</p>
<p>The archive includes texts covering divination, correspondence, rituals and prayers, offering researchers new material for studying Hittite religious practices as well as the development of the Hittite language and scribal culture.</p></blockquote>
	</div><!-- .entry-content -->

	<!-- .entry-footer -->
</article></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Threat of AI share crash puts world financial stability at risk, says IMF (101 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/13/ai-share-crash-risk-global-imf-debt-corrowing-cyber-attacks</link>
            <guid>50067356</guid>
            <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 05:57:32 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/13/ai-share-crash-risk-global-imf-debt-corrowing-cyber-attacks">https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/13/ai-share-crash-risk-global-imf-debt-corrowing-cyber-attacks</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50067356">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="maincontent"><p>The threat of an AI share price crash rippling out across global markets is putting world financial stability at risk, the International Monetary fund has warned.</p><p>In its twice-yearly Global Financial Stability Report, the IMF highlighted the sky-high valuations for AI companies along with their increasing debt pile as one reason that risks in the system are “elevated”.</p><p>“The consequences of an AI correction could be global,” it warned. Aside from the US, Asian markets would be most exposed, and the UK and euro area potentially more insulated – though investors with US holdings would be hit.</p><p>Athanasios Vamvakidis, the deputy director of the IMF’s monetary and capital department, launching the report, said: “The exposure of the rest of the world to US assets, particularly AI, is the highest it has ever been. So this is supporting the global economy through wealth effects today; but if we get a market correction it could have broad implications.”</p><p>An AI crash was one of three “salient risks” to stability identified by the IMF, alongside <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/01/global-bond-sell-off-uk-long-term-borrowing-costs-us-bond-yield" data-link-name="in body link">rising government borrowing costs</a> and the threat of a big cyber-attack.</p><p>It called this trio of factors “plausible near-term catalysts for a sharp tightening of financial conditions and market turmoil”.</p><p>The IMF’s analysis underlined the interconnected nature of big AI companies, and the high levels of borrowing being undertaken to fund investment in the sector.</p><p>“Weaker or less durable investment and profitability in AI could trigger a correction in AI-related asset prices, exacerbated by rising leverage, concentrated exposures, and circular financing arrangements,” it said.</p><p>Such a reassessment would then “sharply reduce investment, trigger equity market corrections, and tighten financial conditions, with global spillovers”.</p><p>The “magnificent seven” big tech stocks – Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Meta, Microsoft, Nvidia and Tesla – account for more than 35% of the S&amp;P 500 share index in the US, up from 12% a decade ago, and their total market capitalisation has increased by more than 50% in the past two years.</p><p>Yet the IMF warned of “growing evidence” that the successful rollout of AI “may not require investment on the massive scale being undertaken by ‘hyperscaler’ firms”, and “potential economic gains may not be as large as expected”.</p><p>The hyperscalers are the companies building the underlying infrastructure for the AI rollout, including many vast datacentres. They include Amazon Web Services, Google, Meta and Oracle.</p><p>Shares in semiconductor companies <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/oct/13/chip-stocks-broadcom-ai-samsung-shares-cxmt" data-link-name="in body link">fell on Tuesday</a> after the boss of Broadcom compared many AI investors to “casino” gamblers. Reports that the Chinese memory chip maker CXMT was making good progress on a new chip architecture also hit South Korean tech companies.</p><p>The IMF also highlighted the growing leverage, or borrowing, that underpins the current boom and could amplify the effects of a crash.</p><figure data-spacefinder-role="inline" data-spacefinder-type="model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.NewsletterSignupBlockElement"><gu-island name="EmailSignUpWrapper" priority="feature" deferuntil="visible" props="{&quot;index&quot;:14,&quot;listId&quot;:4139,&quot;identityName&quot;:&quot;business-today&quot;,&quot;category&quot;:&quot;article-based&quot;,&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Get set for the working day – we'll point you to all the business news and analysis you need every morning&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Business Today&quot;,&quot;frequency&quot;:&quot;Every weekday&quot;,&quot;theme&quot;:&quot;news&quot;,&quot;illustrationSquare&quot;:&quot;https://media.guim.co.uk/e3d43299e85f27e579437406d4cbfb596d9d0c94/0_0_3000_3000/500.jpg&quot;,&quot;exampleUrl&quot;:&quot;/business/series/business-today-newsletter/latest&quot;,&quot;idApiUrl&quot;:&quot;https://idapi.theguardian.com&quot;}"><a data-ignore="global-link-styling" href="#EmailSignup-skip-link-14">skip past newsletter promotion</a><p id="EmailSignup-skip-link-14" tabindex="0" aria-label="after newsletter promotion" role="note">after newsletter promotion</p></gu-island></figure><p>In particular, it identified soaring debts contracted by the companies themselves; hedge fund investment in the sector; and retail investors borrowing to speculate in AI stocks.</p><p>In the event of an AI-led market downturn “these growing sources of leverage heighten the potential for forced selling and surges in equity volatility”, it said.</p><p>It also pointed to the prevalence of <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2025/oct/08/openai-multibillion-dollar-deals-exuberance-circular-nvidia-amd" data-link-name="in body link">“circular financing” deals</a>, tying stronger AI companies to weaker partners in the same sector, and potentially exacerbating the impact of a share price correction.</p><p>It identified $300bn (£227bn) worth of transactions between hyperscalers and “weaker AI firms” in just the past three years, supplemented by $650bn in debt, in exchange for $1.4tn in future financial commitments.</p><p>In London, the chief executive of the UK’s Financial Conduct Authority, Nikhil Rathi, urged every small business across the financial sector to take out insurance to protect themselves against AI-related cybersecurity threats.</p><p>He told MPs on the Commons Treasury committee that not all companies would have the resources to pay for access to “frontier” AI models such as Anthropic’s Mythos, which has been released on a restricted basis after it was said to be rapidly identifying gaps in government and corporate cybersecurity systems.</p><p>“One message I’d give to every small business: you should consider making sure you are insured,” Rathi said.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
