            <title><![CDATA[Who Is Vfmaddrnd231pd? (229 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://purplesyringa.moe/blog/who-is-vfmaddrnd231pd/</link>
            <guid>50063138</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 21:12:43 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://purplesyringa.moe/blog/who-is-vfmaddrnd231pd/">https://purplesyringa.moe/blog/who-is-vfmaddrnd231pd/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50063138">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p><time>October 12, 2026</time></p><p><code>vfmaddrnd231pd</code> is a unique instruction from the FMA instruction set. Most instructions in that set have mnemonics like <code>vfmadd231pd</code>, but here <code>rnd</code> means something very specific. The Intel Software Development Manual documents it as:</p><blockquote><p><strong>VFMADDRND231PD – Fused Multiply-Add of Packed Double-Precision Floating-Point Values with rounding control</strong></p><p>[…] The immediate byte defines several bit fields that control rounding, DAZ, FTZ, and exception suppression (See Table 5-3).</p></blockquote><p>Most x86 floating-point instructions use the global rounding mode and settings, changing which is pretty slow. But <code>vfmaddrnd231pd</code> allows the rounding mode to be controlled on a per-instruction basis, and since FMA can emulate addition, subtraction, and multiplication, this mechanism effectively allows any arithmetic to have controllable rounding without AVX-512 – making some algorithms that rely on rounding incredibly faster.</p><p>Or so it <em>would</em>, if this instruction actually existed. GNU assembler doesn’t recognize it. The Intel C/C++ Compiler intrinsics <code>_mm_fmaddround_pd</code> and <code>_mm256_fmaddround_pd</code> that SDM mentions are absent not only from Clang, but from ICC as well. Trying to assemble the instructions by hand from the SDM opcode list reveals another issue:</p><blockquote><p><code>VEX.DDS.128.66.0F3A.W1 B8 /r /ib</code></p></blockquote><p><code>VEX</code> is the VEX prefix, but what is <code>DDS</code>? This opcode is the only mention of <code>DDS</code> in the SDM, and <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/37354947/5417677">this SO answer</a> says that <code>DDS</code> is merely used for documentation and was removed from later SDM versions, but the fact that it’s still present here is suspicious. And when you actually run the instruction on a CPU that supports the FMA instruction set, like my Haswell, it raises <code>#UD</code> (“UnDefined instruction”)!</p><p>I already found it strange that the SDM only lists <code>vfmaddrnd231pd</code> and not other variants like using <code>pd</code>/<code>ps</code>/<code>sd</code>/<code>ss</code> or <code>132</code>/<code>213</code>/<code>231</code>, which other FMA instructions have, but this confirmed my suspicions. It was at that point that I downloaded a more recent version of the SDM and noticed that this instruction is absent in the 2026 edition of the SDM, despite being present in the 2024 version.</p><p>I must say I’m incredibly confused. This instruction doesn’t look like a typo in the documentation, since the docs contain unique tables and pseudocode that isn’t similar to any other instruction. My best hypothesis is that Intel wanted to add the <code>vfmaddrnd*</code> set, then found an issue with it at the last moment and removed it from the CPU, but didn’t quite adjust the SDM correctly. But I have no way to confirm that.</p><p>If anyone reading this has a friend at Intel, I’d love to know the answer.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
