            <title><![CDATA[Writing a Demoscene Intro for the ZX Spectrum (156 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.markround.com/blog/2026/zx-spectrum-intro</link>
            <guid>50061859</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:40:00 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.markround.com/blog/2026/zx-spectrum-intro">https://www.markround.com/blog/2026/zx-spectrum-intro</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50061859">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
      
        <header>
          
          


          
            <p><em>A look at the tools, techniques and challenges of creating a retro-style 'crack intro'</em></p>
          
          
  <p><strong> Updated:</strong> <time datetime="2026-10-12T05:20:51+00:00">October 12, 2026</time></p>


        </header>
      

      <section itemprop="text">
        


        
        <p><a href="https://www.markround.com/downloads/mdr-tnfstro.zip"><img src="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/cassette.svg" alt="">Download Now</a> <a href="https://www.pouet.net/prod.php?which=107200">View on pouet.net</a> <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50061859">Discussion on Hacker News</a></p>

<details open="">
<summary>
<h2 id="intro">Intro</h2>
</summary>
<div>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/speccy.jpg"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 25vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-400-8abc17021.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-800-8abc17021.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-1200-8abc17021.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 25vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-400-1f31c3383.jpg 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-800-1f31c3383.jpg 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-1200-1f31c3383.jpg 1200w" type="image/jpeg"><img alt="My +3 running my intro" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/speccy-800-1f31c3383.jpg"></picture></a></div>

<p>In the late 80s, I spent an embarrassing number of hours hunched over my ZX Spectrum, typing in Sinclair BASIC programs and rudimentary assembler routines copied from magazines, trying to re-create the Amiga crack intros I’d seen round at a friend’s house. Obviously, they never came even slightly close - but now, many decades later, I’ve finally managed it! I’m now the proud author of a hand-rolled retro demoscene intro that runs on a stock 128Kb Spectrum. It features all those late 80s tropes: A smooth scrolltext (with ‘leet custom fonts!), a three-channel AY soundtrack, bouncing VU meters and the essential parallax starfield. It’s currently 10287 bytes of code, or 19552 bytes as a complete tape image - which, given I’ve been working on this since December 13th last year, means my average rate of code-production is a paltry ~ 455 bytes per week!</p>

<p>This blog post is a technical overview of how I got there, some cool tricks I picked up, and everything I had to learn (and re-learn) about the Speccy and Z80 assembly along the way. It simultaneously rates as one of my favourite side-projects ever, and also the one that most often nearly triggered a foot put through my monitor in frustration. I’ll reflect on all that at <a href="#the-zen-of-z80">the end</a>, but for now if you just want to see the finished product, the download button is at the top of the article and the included <code>.tap</code> cassette tape image should load into any emulator. There’s also a <a href="https://youtu.be/ALbjMMgmAXo">YouTube capture</a>, links to the production on <a href="https://www.pouet.net/prod.php?which=107200">pouet.net</a>, and you can run it from within a web browser by visiting my <a href="https://jsspeccy.markround.com/">TNFS site portal</a> - check option 4 for recent uploads.</p>

<h2 id="be-kind-rewind">Be Kind, Rewind</h2>
<p>But let’s skip back briefly to the late 80s. Coming from the world of an 8-bit, cassette tape loading computer, seeing an Amiga for the first time was like encountering something from outer space. Like everyone who saw one back in the day, I was blown away by the amazing graphics and sound on display, not to mention the library of <a href="https://www.lemonamiga.com/games/votes_list.php">ground-breaking games</a>. But I found myself even more captivated by the mysterious (and totally badass) “intros” displayed at the beginning of pirated games.</p>

<p>Created by the <a href="https://www.kickstarter.com/projects/bitman/bootblock-rebels/">software piracy groups</a> that had cracked the copy protection and distributed the floppy-disk based games, they were mini works of art in their own right - music, graphics and usually some cool-looking effect to show off the group’s prowess. There’s a great collection of these intros (or “cracktros”) <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=50WWFEBsgfk">archived on YouTube</a>.</p>

<p>Watch a few of those videos and you’ll quickly notice the “design” tropes that almost every cracktro shares: a cool, edgy logo at the top of the screen (bonus points if it’s dripping blood or in any way “metal”), some boastful text displayed on top of an effect of some sort - usually a scrolling parallax starfield, or rotating 3d object - and a further scroll-text full of shout outs, greetings to fellow pseudonymous crackers and adverts for illegal BBS systems galore. Needless to say, to a kid in the 80s/early 90s this was possibly the <em>coolest thing ever</em>.</p>

<div role="group" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-label="Gallery">
<div role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="1 of 5">
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/cracktros/horizon.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/horizon-400-b591bdfd4.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/horizon-720-b591bdfd4.webp 720w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/horizon-400-692dc21a9.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/horizon-720-692dc21a9.png 720w" type="image/png"><img alt="I thought this one was the absolute bomb when I first saw it. Sine-scroller, pirate BBS adverts, cool logo with a sword - and of course, a starfield. There’s something for everyone here." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/horizon-720-692dc21a9.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>I thought this one was the absolute <em>bomb</em> when I first saw it. Sine-scroller, pirate BBS adverts, cool logo with a sword - and of course, a starfield. There’s something for everyone here.</p>
</div>
<div role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="2 of 5">
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/cracktros/lemmings.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/lemmings-400-b94ba2f45.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/lemmings-720-b94ba2f45.webp 720w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/lemmings-400-38b76b747.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/lemmings-720-38b76b747.png 720w" type="image/png"><img alt="The music from this is burned into my brain. I think pretty much everyone of my classmates knew this intro given the popularity of Lemmings in the early 90s. Skid Row as a group are still active to this day and this is probably the quintessential Amiga cracktro of the period." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/lemmings-720-38b76b747.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>The music from this is burned into my brain. I think pretty much everyone of my classmates knew this intro given the popularity of Lemmings in the early 90s. Skid Row as a group are still active to this day and this is probably the quintessential Amiga cracktro of the period.</p>
</div>
<div role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="3 of 5">
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/cracktros/piracy.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/piracy-400-867591d44.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/piracy-720-867591d44.webp 720w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/piracy-400-e745a7f08.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/piracy-720-e745a7f08.png 720w" type="image/png"><img alt="Endless Piracy. Edgy. Another cool logo, sine-wave scroller, starfield and boasting text. The classics never go out of style." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/piracy-720-e745a7f08.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>Endless Piracy. Edgy. Another cool logo, sine-wave scroller, starfield and boasting text. The classics never go out of style.</p>
</div>
<div role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="4 of 5">
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/cracktros/spirit.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/spirit-400-c007a2a3c.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/spirit-720-c007a2a3c.webp 720w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/spirit-400-271697994.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/spirit-720-271697994.png 720w" type="image/png"><img alt="Another great combination of all the essential ingredients. Cool logo, even cooler text font, scrolling starfield and a scroll-text running along the bottom. Vintage stuff." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/spirit-720-271697994.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>Another great combination of all the essential ingredients. Cool logo, even cooler text font, scrolling starfield and a scroll-text running along the bottom. Vintage stuff.</p>
</div>
<div role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="5 of 5">
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/cracktros/anthrox.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/anthrox-400-4da314fa6.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/anthrox-720-4da314fa6.webp 720w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 55vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/anthrox-400-b8a1eac02.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/anthrox-720-b8a1eac02.png 720w" type="image/png"><img alt="Logo. Text. Scroller. But no starfield - otherwise a solid effort from Anthrox here :)" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/cracktros/anthrox-720-b8a1eac02.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>Logo. Text. Scroller. But no starfield - otherwise a solid effort from Anthrox here :)</p>
</div>
</div>

<p>The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demoscene">demoscene</a> grew out of these intros. The cracking eventually faded into the background, and coders, “graphicians” and musicians <a href="https://www.datagubbe.se/scenearch/">all contributed</a> to feature-length productions that showcased their abilities, competed with other groups and pushed the machine to its limits. In amazing feats of ingenuity and pure “code as an art form”, the scene lives on today, with groups producing breathtaking productions like <a href="https://youtu.be/2jciCr8zEhw">this</a> and <a href="https://youtu.be/pYtleuGV7ok">this</a> - bear in mind, both of those are running on a 7Mhz home computer equipped with a floppy disk, 1Mb of memory and a sound chip from 1985.</p>

<p>But much as I admire the sheer skill and dedication that takes, it’s the early “single screen” scrolly-text-and-starfield cracktros I’ve always found captivating. They took on a sort of mystical quality for me, hinting at a scene and a culture I could only ever experience from the outside - which is exactly why I spent all those hours in Sinclair BASIC, trying in vain to re-create them, and dreaming of the day I’d eventually have an Amiga of my own.</p>

<h2 id="the-jump-back">The Jump Back</h2>

<p>I eventually did trade up to a range of Amiga systems, joined a few “scene” groups myself and threw myself into a <a href="https://www.markround.com/blog/2026/06/17/25-its-only-when-you-look-back/">variety of activities</a> until exams and the end of my teenage years beckoned. I dabbled with Z80 code back then, and at one point knew enough 68k assembly to bluff my way through - partly from the Amiga’s heyday, partly from a few “embedded systems” modules at University where I had to program a set of traffic lights or lift-controllers. Given there’s not much call for that sort of thing at my $DAYJOB, though, it had all pretty much faded from my memory.</p>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/tnfs.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 25vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-400-f75d78e51.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-800-f75d78e51.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-1200-f75d78e51.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 25vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-400-01940097c.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-800-01940097c.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-1200-01940097c.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="TNFS site screenshot" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/tnfs-800-01940097c.png"></picture></a></div>

<p>I have kept working on a bunch of <a href="https://www.markround.com/projects">projects</a> in my spare time, and one of my favourites is my <a href="https://tnfs.markround.com/">TNFS site</a> - sort of an old-school BBS system or interactive “website” for the Spectrum, which grew out of a <a href="https://www.markround.com/blog/2021/12/21/devops-for-the-sinclair-spectrum-part-1">fun experiment</a> to see how far I could push Sinclair BASIC. It relies on a modern network card fitted to the Spectrum, which is how I came to be chatting to “Desertkun”, another TNFS-protocol enthusiast. He’s now producing a great new <a href="https://spectranext.net/">WiFi network card</a> for the Speccy, and was kind enough to send me a few of the prototypes to test out for him.</p>

<p>I was massively inspired by his hardware and low-level coding chops, and during one of our video calls where we’d been comparing our development setups, he asked why I didn’t write any Z80 assembly myself. A throw-away comment, but the challenge was set! Now, I couldn’t face re-writing the whole site in asm, but I figured I could produce a self-contained intro to promote it in the style of the early “BBStros”, and <em>finally</em> be one of the cool kids with a scene production of my own :)</p>

<p>I mean, how hard could it be ? (<em>ooooh - foreshadowing!</em>)</p>

