<h2 id="dev-environment">Dev Environment</h2>
</summary>
<div>

<p>My development environment through this was pretty straightforward, and is the same stack I use for developing my TNFS site. I just used a Makefile-driven build system which ran my code through the <a href="https://pasmo.speccy.org/">Pasmo</a> assembler, my fork of <code>zmakebas</code> to build BASIC files, and the <a href="https://fuse-emulator.sourceforge.net/">FUSE</a> emulator for testing. For final testing on the real hardware, I uploaded my <code>.tap</code> files to my local TNFS server where I could run them on my +3.</p>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/asm-editor.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-400-c6d1fe5c1.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-800-c6d1fe5c1.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-1200-c6d1fe5c1.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-400-85804483e.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-800-85804483e.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-1200-85804483e.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="ASM Editor screenshot" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-editor-800-85804483e.png"></picture></a></div>

<p>A few additional tools I have to call out because they ended up making a big difference - one is the amazing web-based “asm-editor” I discovered while looking up Z80 instructions. Not only does this site have a great reference of all the Z80 instructions, but it also has an editor where you can run example code and see how the memory and/or various registers change state. Take for example, the <a href="https://asm-editor.specy.app/documentation/z80/instruction/djnz">djnz</a> instruction - you can build it and step through the example loop construct one instruction at a time. Or try my example of an <a href="https://asm-editor.specy.app/documentation/z80/instruction/add">add</a> instruction in the screenshot to see how the carry flag works when a bit gets shifted out of a register. Although it uses a slightly different syntax to Pasmo (so I couldn’t drop my code directly in it), it massively helped when manually stepping through things.</p>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/dogs.jpg"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 30vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-400-bb0aa7364.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-800-bb0aa7364.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-1200-bb0aa7364.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 30vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-400-e1a0c22d8.jpg 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-800-e1a0c22d8.jpg 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-1200-e1a0c22d8.jpg 1200w" type="image/jpeg"><img alt="Best debugging agents ever." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/dogs-800-e1a0c22d8.jpg"></picture></a></div>

<p>And lastly, probably the most significant debugging tools I used on this project - a notepad and pen, and my two dogs (say “Hi!” to Biscuit &amp; Pebble…) I’ll write some more about this in the <a href="#the-zen-of-z80">wrap-up</a>, but this really was a highly unusual project for me, coming from decades of much higher-level languages and constructs. Most of my z80 code I only ever really understood after drawing out the bytes and bitmasks, keeping a table of register flags and drawing chunks of screen memory out by hand. And I found my evening walks with the two dogs provided ideal <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubber_duck_debugging">rubber duck</a> exercises, although I’m not sure what they both made of my protracted mutterings about the <code>hl</code> register pair. Either way, debugging-by-dog is highly recommended!</p>

