<h2 id="the-starting-point">The Starting Point</h2>
</summary>
<div>
<p>So, let’s get started. I followed a great “Hello, world!” example from <a href="https://www.futurice.com/blog/build-environment-like-its-1986">futurice.com</a> to validate my build environment. Beyond that, the first thing I wanted to do was load in my logo and background image I’d use as a “stage” for the remainder of the planned intro. I wasn’t really sure what I was going to do at this point, just that I wanted my logo at the top, and a scrolltext along the bottom row of the screen. As my TNFS site header already looked kinda demoscene-y, I made a quick edit putting in place two blue guide-bars inside which I’d put further content.</p>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/memory_map.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 30vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/memory_map-400-40079e071.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/memory_map-452-40079e071.webp 452w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 30vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/memory_map-400-cba2ad4fc.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/memory_map-452-cba2ad4fc.png 452w" type="image/png"><img alt="TNFS site screenshot" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/memory_map-452-cba2ad4fc.png"></picture></a></div>

<p>A Spectrum <code>SCREEN$</code> file (usually displayed as loading screens) is just a dump of screen memory, starting from byte 16384, and running for 6912 bytes. The weird layout of this is very relevant later, but in order to display it all you need to do is load your file into that memory location. After a few hour’s work of reviewing Speccy internals and following various guides, I had a very simple starting point for my demo that served as the basis for everything else that came after.</p>

<div><pre><code><span>org</span> <span>$8000</span>

<span>screen</span>  <span>EQU</span> <span>$4000</span> <span>; address of the first byte of screen memory, 16384 in dec</span>
<span>scrlen</span>  <span>EQU</span> <span>6912</span>  <span>; total length of the screen memory inc. attributes</span>
<span>ula</span>     <span>EQU</span> <span>$FE</span>   <span>; I/O port for the ULA to control the border</span>
<span>black</span>   <span>EQU</span> <span>0</span>     <span>; Spectrum colour number, the exact same ones BASIC uses</span>

<span>start</span><span>:</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>a</span><span>,</span><span>black</span>      <span>; Set the border colour to black using the ULA</span>
  <span>out</span> <span>(</span><span>ula</span><span>),</span><span>a</span>

  <span>; Copy my logo header picture to screen memory</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>hl</span><span>,</span><span>header</span>    <span>; HL = location of my screen file in this code</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>de</span><span>,</span><span>screen</span>    <span>; DE = start of screen memory</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>bc</span><span>,</span><span>scrlen</span>    <span>; BC = 6912 bytes to copy</span>
  <span>ldir</span>            <span>; block copy, see https://jnz.dk/z80/ldir.html</span>

<span>; busy loop - rest of intro goes here :P                    </span>
<span>hold</span><span>:</span>
  <span>halt</span>            <span>; sleep until the next interrupt (1/50 second)</span>
  <span>jr</span> <span>hold</span>         <span>; then jump back and sleep again, for ever</span>

<span>header</span><span>:</span>
  <span>incbin</span> <span>"assets/header.scr"</span>  <span>; my logo header/background</span>

<span>end</span> <span>start</span>
</code></pre></div>

<div>
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/screen.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 50vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-400-0d42201f6.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-800-0d42201f6.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-1200-0d42201f6.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 50vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-400-6ec15b2c9.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-800-6ec15b2c9.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-1200-6ec15b2c9.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="Not much to look at now, but this simple starting point introduced me to important concepts like interfacing with the Spectrum’s hardware ( ULA chip to start with, and AY sound chip later) via the out instruction, how to pull in binary files using Pasmo’s INCBIN directive, using the Z80’s block copy ldir instruction, the use of register pairs for 16-bit addressing, and simple branching to labels." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/screen-800-6ec15b2c9.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>Not much to look at now, but this simple starting point introduced me to important concepts like interfacing with the Spectrum’s hardware (<a href="https://sinclair.wiki.zxnet.co.uk/wiki/ZX_Spectrum_ULA">ULA</a> chip to start with, and AY sound chip later) via the <code>out</code> instruction, how to pull in binary files using Pasmo’s <a href="https://pasmo.speccy.org/pasmodoc.html#dirincbin">INCBIN</a> directive, using the Z80’s block copy <code>ldir</code> instruction, the use of <a href="https://jnz.dk/z80/registers.html">register pairs</a> for 16-bit addressing, and simple branching to labels.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="hello-world">Hello World</h3>
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/hello.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-400-a2b82eec3.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-800-a2b82eec3.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-1200-a2b82eec3.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-400-d0d7c965a.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-800-d0d7c965a.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-1200-d0d7c965a.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/hello-800-d0d7c965a.png"></picture></a></div>

<p>The next step was to print something on the screen and explore some of the Speccy’s built-in ROM routines, which are somewhat analagous to syscalls on modern operating systems. There are plenty of disassemblies and guides to the Spectrum ROM available on the internet, one of the best commented and linked one is at <a href="https://skoolkid.github.io/rom/">https://skoolkid.github.io/rom/</a>. Using this as a reference alongside the various “Hello, world!” tutorials showed me how this sort of thing is built up:</p>

<div><pre><code>  <span>at</span>      <span>EQU</span> <span>22</span> <span>; "at" control character used to position the cursor</span>
  <span>row</span>     <span>EQU</span> <span>11</span> <span>; coordinates where to print the message</span>
  <span>col</span>     <span>EQU</span> <span>9</span>

  <span>prstring</span> <span>EQU</span> <span>$203C</span> <span>; where the pr_string routine lives in ROM</span>

  <span>ld</span> <span>de</span><span>,</span><span>msg</span>        <span>; DE = address of the first character</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>bc</span><span>,</span><span>msglen</span>     <span>; BC = how many characters to print</span>
  <span>call</span> <span>prstring</span>    <span>; print the lot</span>

<span>; busy loop is the same...</span>

<span>msg</span><span>:</span>
  <span>db</span> <span>at</span><span>,</span><span>row</span><span>,</span><span>col</span>            <span>; equivalent of "PRINT AT 11,9" in BASIC</span>
  <span>db</span> <span>"Hello, world!"</span>
<span>msglen</span>  <span>EQU</span> <span>$</span><span>-</span><span>msg</span>          <span>; 16 bytes in all, pasmo shortcut, $=program counter</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>I ended up mostly not using the ROM routines in my code as they dragged in all sorts of things I didn’t need, but it was massively useful to understand <em>how</em> they worked as they introduced me to yet more commonly-used constructs. For example, the <code>PR_STRING</code> routine defined <a href="https://skoolkid.github.io/rom/asm/1FFC.html">here</a> shows how you can build a loop over each character. If you’re just starting out, I highly recommend going over the 8 lines in that routine with a notepad and pen, working out the contents of the registers and following the loop constructs.</p>

<div>
<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/asm-prtstring.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 80vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-400-17e39d620.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-800-17e39d620.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-1200-17e39d620.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 80vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-400-50a5d8c2a.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-800-50a5d8c2a.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-1200-50a5d8c2a.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="I also made a version of it in asm-editor you can try. If you click that link, you’ll see a version of the code loaded into the web editor. Click “Build”, and then step through the code using the “Step” button - although no text will be actually displayed (I skipped over the call to the Spectrum ROM “restart” vector that does this), you’ll see the various states of the registers and for example the BC register counting down as each byte of the string is processed." src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/asm-prtstring-800-50a5d8c2a.png"></picture></a></div>
<p>I also made a version of it in <a href="https://asm-editor.specy.app/projects/share?-P7tQnEmDUFUAK0kVrJ9BFcwKRRWzOw1GFPg4sTiHEy30CmnMFRSHFyAfE4ZFIAAEjBiZQdvbJBsAITKECqEAAXyrXAEkkRjHQWDwRE4IDgMF4vHoCGQte41x87tMgkQKQA7LlJxEAKyEKIAZlyi64oSY1GH1FHSEn04nc8Ii4iE4PXG8nUYugA+gBlIz6AwAcSEMDkaBQLHplEoTHbrApC1ekYAAtNsuC2OByBxJge0odhEBAUwfh-JgMX1GAUHgslh1heF7hve9HxfW8mBgexJFCFAhFiUIe0wdoiwAawQLhfF4QlOz7ABtABdLgIGoRhr2vOBXhgETqyAA">asm-editor</a> you can try. If you click that link, you’ll see a version of the code loaded into the web editor. Click “Build”, and then step through the code using the “Step” button - although no text will be actually displayed (I skipped over the call to the Spectrum ROM “restart” vector that does this), you’ll see the various states of the registers and for example the BC register counting down as each byte of the string is processed.</p>
