<h3 id="adding-some-style">Adding Some Style</h3>
<p>Problem is, the Speccy’s built-in font is not exactly dripping with demoscene credibility. I wanted to use some stylish fonts and this turned out to be super easy - a quick search turned up Jim Blimey’s awesome <a href="https://www.jimblimey.com/blog/24-zx-spectrum-fonts/">font collection</a>, which included a ready-to-use set of assembler files and some instructions which soon led me to these additions in my code:</p>

<div><pre><code><span>; CHARS is the address the ROM's printing routine reads to find the font. </span>
<span>; Picking our own font is simply a matter of over-writing this</span>
<span>chars</span>   <span>EQU</span> <span>$5C36</span> <span>; from Jim's docs - POKE 23607=5C36 in hex</span>

  <span>ld</span> <span>hl</span><span>,</span><span>myfont</span><span>-</span><span>256</span> <span>; HL = font address - 256 as per docs</span>
  <span>ld</span> <span>(</span><span>chars</span><span>),</span><span>hl</span>    <span>; from now on the ROM draws with it!</span>

<span>; rest of code is the same</span>

<span>myfont</span><span>:</span>
  <span>include</span> <span>"textfont.asm"</span> <span>; "Cartoon" font from jimblimey.com</span>
</code></pre></div>

<div><a href="https://www.markround.com/assets/images/intro/font.png"><picture><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-400-6bc654bd3.webp 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-800-6bc654bd3.webp 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-1200-6bc654bd3.webp 1200w" type="image/webp"><source sizes="(max-width: 599px) 100vw, 40vw" srcset="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-400-13482085f.png 400w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-800-13482085f.png 800w, https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-1200-13482085f.png 1200w" type="image/png"><img alt="" src="https://www.markround.com/assets/generated/intro/font-800-13482085f.png"></picture></a></div>

<p>There we go! Now that’s looking a little more elite. Sorry, I mean <code>31337</code>. Strap in folks, we’re leaving lamer city… So at this point, a month or so in to my dabblings and I’d started to get a reasonable understanding of common idioms and was starting to feel like this was a project that might actually go somewhere. The problem started when I moved on to trying to implement actual demo effects: It became very apparent, very quickly, just how much I <em>didn’t</em> know about how this all worked. Not to mention the fact that at this level, you’re really closely exposed to the physical hardware intricacies, including the design decisions and trade-offs that the hardware designers had to make when working with the extremely limited specs of the early 1980s.</p>

