            <title><![CDATA[Making big downloads resumable in Firefox (241 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://lunarwatcher.codeberg.page/til/firefox/resumable-big-downloads.html</link>
            <guid>50061791</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:36:02 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://lunarwatcher.codeberg.page/til/firefox/resumable-big-downloads.html">https://lunarwatcher.codeberg.page/til/firefox/resumable-big-downloads.html</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50061791">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
            
    
    <div>
    <div>
        <p><img src="https://lunarwatcher.codeberg.page/assets/profile.png" alt="Profile picture"></p>
    </div>
    
        <p><span>firefox</span>
            
        </p>
    
</div>

<hr>

    <p>For whatever reason, Firefox 28 and newer (i.e. every modern version after like <a href="https://morgb.blogspot.com/2014/05/firefox-29-and-http-response-timeout.html">2014</a>) ships with <code>network.http.response.timeout</code> set to 300 seconds. This seems to interfere with resuming big downloads, as well as the general stability of big downloads.</p><p>Setting <code>network.http.response.timeout</code> to <code>0</code> (or something very big) in <code>about:config</code> seems to make big downloads both resumable and at least a little more stable. I don't understand why this works, but experimental testing supports it.</p><p>Note that this assumes the server supports resumable downloads; if it doesn't, no settings in firefox or any other browser are going to help. However, the problem this fixes is firefox being unable to resume a resumable download on error. I don't entirely know if this is the case, but it experimentally looks like resumable downloads in firefox rely on connection timeouts rather than the <code>.part</code> files they download. Firefox will happily ignore an existing <code>.part</code> without this option, and I do not understand why.</p><p>Resumable downloads in firefox generally appear to be fairly unstable though. This has been a known problem in some form since <a href="https://forums.mozillazine.org/viewtopic.php?t=238764">as far back as 2005</a>. I also found that there's <a href="https://superuser.com/a/1103144">a manual <code>.part</code> shuffle to make firefox actually resume downloads</a>, but I'm not doing that shit. As a non-firefox workaround, using <code>wget -c -O "$filename" "$url"</code> also works, and wget overall appears to be fairly stable.</p><p>I do also suspect firefox does some hidden retries if it can before it considers the download to have failed - from some experimental testing, it does appear that this is at least partly related to <code>network.http.response.timeout</code>, and that exceeding the timeout interferes with this process.</p><p>I have not attempted to confirm any of this with FF's source code, because I have no clue where to look.</p>
    


        </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
