            <title><![CDATA[Codex CLI installs a persistent, self-updating background daemon by default (577 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48195</link>
            <guid>50060694</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:29:04 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48195">https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48195</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060694">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-testid="markdown-body" data-team-hovercards-enabled="true" data-turbolinks="false"><h3 dir="auto">What variant of Codex are you using?</h3>
<p dir="auto">CLI — 0.157.0, installed via <code>npm install -g @openai/codex</code>, Linux (WSL2)</p>
<h3 dir="auto">What feature would you like to see?</h3>
<p dir="auto">Please make <code>daemon_auto_start</code> <strong>opt-in (default <code>false</code>) for the CLI</strong>, or at least ask once before turning it on.</p>
<p dir="auto">Since 0.157.0, just opening an interactive <code>codex</code> session:</p>
<ol dir="auto">
<li><strong>Starts a persistent background daemon</strong> (<code>codex app-server --managed-daemon</code>). It runs in its own session with no TTY and keeps running after every Codex window is closed (reparented to PID 1) until <code>codex app-server daemon stop</code> or a reboot.</li>
<li><strong>Copies the whole platform package</strong> (~374 MB: <code>codex</code>, <code>codex-code-mode-host</code>, bundled <code>bwrap</code>/<code>zsh</code>/<code>rg</code>) into <code>~/.codex/packages/app-server-daemon/releases/&lt;version&gt;/</code> — byte-identical to what npm had just installed — and runs the daemon from that copy. From then on the backend's version is decoupled from the CLI version I installed with my package manager.</li>
<li><strong>Starts a detached updater</strong> (<code>codex app-server daemon pid-update-loop</code>) that checks for updates 5 minutes after start and then hourly, downloads new releases and restarts the backend (which can kill active turns, see <span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5261194661" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/40969" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/40969/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/40969">app-server daemon: auto-update force-kills active turns after a 60s drain budget, and cannot be disabled<span>&nbsp;#40969</span></a></span>).</li>
<li><strong>Pins the environment of whichever session happened to start it.</strong> I launch Codex through a wrapper that sets <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> per invocation; the daemon keeps the first session's proxy variables and its outbound connections go through that proxy, whatever later sessions are launched with. <span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5332806935" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/42457" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/42457/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/42457">Hooks in --remote mode receive the daemon's cwd, not the session's<span>&nbsp;#42457</span></a></span> and <span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5095920473" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/37537" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/37537/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/37537">Hooks spawned by the app-server daemon cannot identify the client terminal, breaking terminal-targeting hooks<span>&nbsp;#37537</span></a></span> (daemon cwd / terminal instead of the session's) look like the same root cause.</li>
</ol>
<p dir="auto">For a CLI, the least surprising default is that the backend lives and dies with the window, runs the binary I installed, and doesn't update itself in the background. A shared, self-updating daemon makes sense for Remote Control / the desktop app, so it would be fine to enable it there, or when the user explicitly runs <code>codex app-server daemon start</code> / <code>bootstrap</code>.</p>
<p dir="auto">If it stays on by default, please at least:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>exit an auto-started daemon when its last client disconnects;</li>
<li>not copy/self-update when the CLI was installed by a package manager (npm/brew/pacman) — run the installed binary instead (<span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5271410146" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/41188" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/41188/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/41188">Support externally managed app-server daemon executables<span>&nbsp;#41188</span></a></span>, <span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5503280565" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/46468" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/46468/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/46468">npm update leaves app-server on old version, but daemon update/restart refuse recovery<span>&nbsp;#46468</span></a></span>, <span><a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5581326709" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48050" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/48050/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48050">Codex 0.157.0 no longer starts with the Arch Linux package unless --no-daemon is used<span>&nbsp;#48050</span></a></span>).</li>
</ul>
<h3 dir="auto">Additional information</h3>
<p dir="auto"><strong>Bug found while turning it off:</strong> <code>codex app-server daemon stop</code> stops the app-server but leaves the <code>pid-update-loop</code> updater running (still alive 70 s later, had to kill it manually).</p>
<p dir="auto">Workaround I'm using:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="codex features disable daemon_auto_start
codex app-server daemon stop
pkill -f 'app-server daemon pid-update-loop'   # not stopped by `daemon stop`
rm -rf ~/.codex/packages/app-server-daemon"><pre>codex features disable daemon_auto_start
codex app-server daemon stop
pkill -f <span><span>'</span>app-server daemon pid-update-loop<span>'</span></span>   <span><span>#</span> not stopped by `daemon stop`</span>
rm -rf <span>~</span>/.codex/packages/app-server-daemon</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">With the flag off, a new TUI session no longer spawns a daemon.</p>
<p dir="auto">Related: <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5261194661" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/40969" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/40969/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/40969">#40969</a>, <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5271410146" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/41188" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/41188/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/41188">#41188</a>, <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5503280565" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/46468" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/46468/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/46468">#46468</a>, <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5581326709" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48050" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/48050/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/48050">#48050</a>, <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5332806935" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/42457" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/42457/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/42457">#42457</a>, <a data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="5095920473" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/37537" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/openai/codex/issues/37537/hovercard" href="https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/37537">#37537</a></p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Publishers Killed the Paperback, Then Blamed Readers (133 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://jacobin.com/2026/10/books-publishing-reading-mass-market-paperback</link>
            <guid>50060674</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:27:26 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://jacobin.com/2026/10/books-publishing-reading-mass-market-paperback">https://jacobin.com/2026/10/books-publishing-reading-mass-market-paperback</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060674">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="post-content"><div><p><span>I</span>t seems every week a new piece about the decline and fall of the American reading habit goes viral. The idea that we’re entering a literary apocalypse that threatens to destabilize our democracy is perhaps overstated, but nobody is denying the book-reading trend is grim. In the most recent <a href="https://nces.ed.gov/pubs2019/2019179/index.asp">international assessment</a> of adult skills, conducted in 2023, 28 percent of American adults scored at or below the lowest level of literacy&nbsp;— up from 19 percent just six years earlier. By one widely cited estimate, more than half of American adults read below a sixth-grade level.</p><p>It’s interesting to note that these hand-wringing, sky-is-falling pieces unfailingly focus on external issues. Few of us doubt what we feel in our bones to be true&nbsp;— that long-form reading is being harmed by disruptive technologies and other beyond-our-control societal issues (political dysfunction and a decrepit educational system) that apply to the demand side of the equation.</p><p>But what about the supply side? Could it be that corporatized publishing&nbsp;— with all of its consolidations and centralization&nbsp;— may have enshittified the product to the point where it has abetted the decline in consumer demand?</p></div><div id="ch-1"><p><span>C</span>onsider the death of the mass-market paperback last year. When the Big Five publishing houses (Penguin Random House, Macmillan, HarperCollins, Hachette, and Simon &amp; Schuster) took the most affordable book format in America&nbsp;— known for its modest price and compact size&nbsp;— off of life support in 2025, it had long ago failed to live up to the “mass” part of its name. At that point, the books under that banner were recognizable but lacking the vitality and freshness that had first driven them to national attention in the twentieth century.</p><p>Until the 1940s, book retail was dominated by hardcovers&nbsp;— books held between cloth- or paper-covered boards, the pages secured by sewn spines. <a href="https://www.penguin.co.uk/about/company-articles/celebrating-sir-allen-lanes-life-and-legacy">Penguin</a> in the UK and Pocket Books here in the United States were the first to make successful use of an alternative book format that rivaled&nbsp;— and, for many decades, far outsold&nbsp;— the hardback. This cheaper-to-manufacture book had a soft cover and relied upon a glued (versus stitched) binding. The resulting tome was easier to carry and <em>much</em> easier to afford. The new format was as much commercial opportunism as it was any attempt to democratize reading or to improve the citizenry’s understanding of the world. If you make a dime on each purchase of one hundred thousand paperbacks, that’s more than making a buck each on 2,500 hardcovers, which was the <a href="https://archive.org/details/twobitculturepap00davi">average unit sale</a> back then. Better yet, these books were sold all over middle America&nbsp;— in drugstores, grocery stores, and gas stations&nbsp;— not just in the little metropolitan oases.</p><figure><img width="834" src="https://media.jacobin.com/images/2026/10/611836574905.png" loading="lazy" height="1089" alt="A 1939 ad for mass market paperbacks in The New York Times"><figcaption>A 1939 ad for mass-market paperbacks in <cite>The New York Times</cite></figcaption></figure><p>In the post–Great Depression years, American bookstores were hardly ubiquitous. Large metro areas with a sufficient intelligentsia density might have a few, but in the 1930s there were only <a href="https://archive.org/details/twobitculturepap00davi">about five hundred</a> dedicated bookshops across all of America. At the same time, basic literacy was nearly universal&nbsp;— by the 1930 census, some 96 percent of Americans over the age of ten could read and write&nbsp;— but the average American only knew books through schools (which used to buy a lot more books than they do now), libraries (which used to contain a lot more books than they do now), and catalogs (which used to sell a lot more books than they do now). Unless you lived in an affluent area with a bookstore or were near a department store or gift shop&nbsp;— which also sometimes had small book sections&nbsp;— you simply did not encounter them as a working adult.</p><p>But when these cheap little books dubbed “mass-market paperbacks” began showing up, they were a hit. America’s Pocket Books launched in 1939 and sold 8.5 million copies within two years. Which is even more remarkable considering they were <a href="https://archive.org/details/twobitculturepap00davi">not yet being sold</a> “north of Boston, west of Chicago, or south of Washington [DC].” The books were <a href="https://archive.org/details/twobitculturepap00davi">averaging</a> close to one hundred thousand units each, with a population a little over a third the size of today’s United States. When the industry discontinued the format in 2024, the highest-selling new mass-market title&nbsp;— a <a href="https://www.publishersweekly.com/pw/by-topic/industry-news/publisher-news/article/97179-book-publishers-plan-for-a-reduced-mass-market-paperback-footprint.html">Danielle Steel</a> book&nbsp;— sold 53,000 units. It’s no wonder the executives decided to scuttle the whole thing and to blame the customer for it.</p></div><div id="ch-2"><p><span>O</span>ne of the bigger recent pieces on the supposed new trend in anti-literacy was an <cite>Atlantic</cite> <a href="https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/2026/08/reading-crisis-postliterate-age/687618/">piece</a> called “The End of Reading Is Here,” and it was typical in its emphasis on the failures of the society that is degenerating outside the walls of the Big Five. The piece gives us the history of publishing from Mesopotamia to Ptolemy to Johannes Gutenberg and emphasizes the importance that our nation’s founders and civil war heroes placed on long-form print.</p><p>Marshall McLuhan, Neil Postman, Walter J. Ong, and Alexander Luria are name-checked. The tech-happy media and educational industries are decried, and the system that allows kids to spend 4.5 hours a day on social media is called out, but nowhere in the massive piece is any shade thrown at the book publishing industry with whom <i>The</i> <cite>Atlantic</cite>, for 169 years, has been cozy, and to which the article’s Yale-educated writer likely&nbsp;— as with most staff writers&nbsp;— means to sell manuscripts over the course of a career.</p><p>There is plenty of good reason for gloom. We are told that 80 percent of elementary school teachers report that their students have, by kindergarten, received school-issued <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2025/1/12/upshot/teachers-survey-chromebooks-class.html">personal video devices</a>. But the <cite>Atlantic</cite> piece contains no implication that one possible reason books and long-form reading are falling away, a theoretically contributory factor to the mass market’s extinction and the plummeting demand for books, involves the supply-side of the equation. Any thought that <em>what</em> the publishing industry is printing, or <em>how</em> it has been pricing it, or <em>where</em> it has been placing it, just might have harmed our nation’s reading habit is never mentioned. The writer has good company in blaming the people on the outside. None of the publishing executives and pundits that attended the mass-market paperback’s funeral ceremonies last year did any self-implication either.</p><p>“We follow the consumer,” said Dennis Abboud, CEO of ReaderLink, America’s largest book distributor, upon announcing the death of the mass-market paperback. “It seems the consumer has spoken,” said Steve Zacharius, CEO of one of the biggest publishers in the format. “As a format, I would say it’s pretty much over,” said Ivan Held of Penguin Random House. All this shrugging and hand-washing for the book format that, as one regretful publisher eloquently put it, saved (or at least forestalled the decline of) America. “I believe that mass-market paperbacks democratized America,” said Esther Margolis of Newmarket Press. “For basically the equivalent of a dollar or two, you know, you could be educated. You could pick them up at the school book fair. You could pick them up at a local gas station&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;.&nbsp;You didn’t have to be an elitist.”</p><p>But could the mega-consolidated Big Five American publishing industry bear any blame for this state of affairs?</p><p>Consumer scientists have long understood that that popular habits are not just among the hardest things to break but are also very difficult to make. Many companies try to integrate their products into preexisting habits, but it’s rare that a company touches off an entirely new one, much less one at the scale of the mass-market paperback. Those 8.5 million paperbacks sold by Pocket Books from 1939 to 1941 were arguably proof of a bigger, more instantaneous habit birth than that of even the <a href="https://prosperion.us/commentary/the-impact-of-iphone-5/">iPhone</a>, which sold a comparable amount of their own product but to a population that had grown three times larger.</p><p>But America’s decades-long mass-market paperback binge (which in 1979 topped out at <a href="https://www.publishersweekly.com/pw/by-topic/industry-news/publisher-news/article/99293-last-call-for-mass-market-paperbacks.html">387 million</a> annual units sold) suggests that we, as a people, are capable of&nbsp;— and perhaps even prone to&nbsp;— falling into a book-reading habit. So was there anything publishers could have done to prevent the death of the mass-market paperback and the decline of our national reading habit?</p></div><div id="ch-3"><p><span>I</span>n 1939, television didn’t exist, movies lasted only a couple hours&nbsp;— versus the days you can occupy through a single book&nbsp;— and even there you were stuck with whatever was playing at the non-multiplex cinema. You also had at best a dozen radio stations to choose from versus the cheap time-killing options we have on this side of the digital revolution.</p><p>The new landscape obviously comprises external demand-affecting features that exist beyond the reach of even the most powerful book industry executive. And judging whether books are more or less attractive seems like a hard one to quantify. Proving that publishers have gotten better or worse at designing attractive book jackets, executing motivating marketing campaigns, and delivering compelling manuscripts would doubtless be largely subjective.</p><p>But one aspect of appeal for which it is much easier to hold the publishers’ feet to the fire is range. There is a bigger story to be told here, but let’s take a snapshot and consider the <a href="https://archive.org/details/twobitculturepap00davi">first ten</a> mass-market paperbacks that Pocket Books published:</p><figure><img width="1200" src="https://media.jacobin.com/images/2026/10/630861804558.png" loading="lazy" height="1172" alt="An image featuring ten mass market paperbacks from 1939"></figure><p>Emily Brontë, <cite>Bambi</cite>, Dorothy Parker, Agatha Christie, Shakespeare, a Victorian bildungsroman and a Pulitzer Prize–winning novel by Thornton Wilder. That’s quite a broad spectrum! Now let’s hold this mini-catalog up against the mass-market <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/books/best-sellers/2025/02/23/mass-market-monthly/">bestsellers list</a> from the very month in 2025 that Dennis Abboud, CEO of ReaderLink&nbsp;— a former managing partner at a private equity firm&nbsp;— announced that the mass-market paperback was to be put out to pasture:</p><figure><img width="1200" src="https://media.jacobin.com/images/2026/10/060305766249.png" loading="lazy" height="1386" alt="An image featuring the ten best-selling mass market paperbacks of 2026"></figure><p>Comparing the first ten paperbacks ever published&nbsp;— which sold millions of copies in a very different world&nbsp;— to the last ten is not entirely unfair. Those were the industry’s ten priority titles of 1939; the latter ones are the ten priority titles of the industry eighty-six years later. In other words, decisions&nbsp;— curations, really&nbsp;— made by the companies. The former are effectively the ten books a person of 1939 was most likely to have encountered while shopping, and the latter are the ten books a person is most likely to have encountered while doing the same in February 2025. In 1939, we have adventure books, plays, poems, a story about a deer, a murder mystery, a prize-winning literary novel, and a movie tie-in&nbsp;— all by a set of authors ranging in age, if they were living at time of publication, from thirty-eight to sixty-nine. In 2025, we have only contemporary commercial fiction options, half of which are series titles (one of which had a whopping thirty-five prior installments) and by eight brand-name authors, average age seventy-four, plus two whose franchise author is a man who would have been eighty-six at the time of publication, at least had he not died in 2004.</p><p>There’s nothing wrong with pursuing safe bets: all these old brand authors and their long-established readerships are obviously dependable, at least from a quarterly earnings standpoint, versus Pocket Books’ unscientific, consumer-data-lacking, instinct-driven&nbsp;— albeit extremely successful&nbsp;— strategy from 1939. But given that variety is an important part of attracting consumers, is it possible our twenty-first century publishers have diminished the wider appeal of their product&nbsp;— for readers and potential readers outside the dependable boomer commercial-fiction core&nbsp;— even just a little bit?</p></div><div id="ch-4"><p><span>A</span>s to affordability, ¢25 (the price of each of Pocket Books’ titles) went a lot further in 1939 than it does now but was still a consumer-friendly price point. It was just slightly higher than what our great-great-grandparents would have paid for a gallon of gas and slightly lower than what they’d have paid for a dozen eggs. In 2024, the format’s last year of full national distribution, a new mass-market paperback would set you back three times as much as a dozen eggs or a gallon of gas.</p><p>One might suspect that materials, distribution, or other book-specific costs had risen over the intervening decades. But how then to explain that the mass-market paperback’s older sibling&nbsp;— the hidebound hardcover&nbsp;— hasn’t seen an inflation nearly so dramatic? Today’s hardback novel has a cover price between $28 and $32. That’s 40 percent cheaper than it would have been back in the 1940s, and this is before a typical national retailer discount of more than 20 percent (and often up to 50 percent) for best-selling titles. In 1939, you could buy ten mass-market paperbacks for the cost of a single new hardback. In 2024, you could barely buy two. Which means that under the leadership of the Big Five and their equally hegemonic national retail partners, the flagship product favored by elite-facing bookstores in wealthy enclaves got cheaper, and the format with the proven mass potential got many times more expensive.</p><p>Finally, let’s consider availability. Even before the mass-market paperback was discontinued, books were being carried in fewer and fewer places. Supermarkets, drugstores, truck stops, newsstands, and department stores,had all gotten out of the business. And the bookselling stalwarts of recent decades have been faltering too. In 2024, Costco, which used to sell <a href="https://janefriedman.com/costco-pulls-back-on-stocking-books/">millions</a> of units a year, <a href="https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/2024/12/01/costco-book-sales/76689057007/">stopped</a> carrying books in most of its stores. The big national book retailer Borders, with an assist from <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2011/jul/01/borders-agrees-sell-itself-najafi">private equity</a>, went out of business in 2011. Barnes &amp; Noble has been roughly holding steady, but it has just around six hundred outlets in at best 19 percent of our nation’s counties.</p><figure><img width="643" src="https://media.jacobin.com/images/2026/10/212930752273.jpg" loading="lazy" height="857" alt="A spinner rack in a Canadian drugstore, July 2026 "><figcaption>A spinner rack in a Canadian drugstore, July 2026 (Courtesy Ned Rust)</figcaption></figure><p>The trends the publishers and the media cite to justify this availability crisis are real. But were they unavoidable? And are they excusable? And are they perhaps also driven by problems the two other As&nbsp;— appeal and affordability&nbsp;— over which the industry has even more direct control? Could and should the US publishing industry in fact be visibly exploring solutions or putting up a public fight to maintain a more significant non-digital distribution of its titles? Could and should the industry notice that the mass-market paperback is still for sale in Canada&nbsp;— where, in that same 2023 assessment, just 18 percent of adults scored at or below the lowest literacy level, compared with 28 percent here&nbsp;— and at a price less than half of what it rose to in the United States?</p><p>It’s difficult to see how the corporatized, highly centralized industry is walking away blameless from killing off one of its former darlings&nbsp;— the affordable paperback. Clearly it’s easier to focus on fewer higher-income readers than on a multitude of Americans whose lives you have no social connection to. Clearly decisions were made&nbsp;— ones that no doubt have had a major effect on American reading habits. And clearly the highly intertwined media establishment is not going to second-guess them.</p><p>But maybe you should.</p></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Just say no to OpenAI and Anthropic (160 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://micahflee.com/just-say-no-to-openai-and-anthropic/</link>
            <guid>50060500</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:17:29 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://micahflee.com/just-say-no-to-openai-and-anthropic/">https://micahflee.com/just-say-no-to-openai-and-anthropic/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060500">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div>
        <p>I recently read <a href="https://naomiklein.org/end-times-fascism/" rel="noreferrer"><em>End Times Fascism and the Fight for the Living World</em></a> by Naomi Klein and Astra Taylor. It's an excellent book and I highly recommend that everyone read it. Tech oligarchs make up an important component of the end-times-fascist coalition. One thing that stood out to me, as a software and security engineer who finds LLMs to be extremely powerful, is that the American frontier labs are run by unhinged lunatics who genuinely believe they're creating the Machine God, that they're fine with burning down the world to bring about AGI, and that they believe with religious fervor that they're the only ones smart enough to safely control it. I already kind of knew this, but this book really made it sink in.</p><p>As much as the AI hype all around us pretends that OpenAI and Anthropic are unique and special, they're not. They're not the only companies doing this work. In fact, a lot of the open weight models are just about as good as the latest ChatGPT and Claude models, including for incredibly complex tasks like vulnerability research.</p><p>Unlike models from OpenAI and Anthropic, open weight models have major advantages:</p><ul><li>It's possible to run them privately on your own hardware (if you can somehow get a computer with enough RAM)</li><li>You can use a provider like Tinfoil that relies on secure enclaves to run them privately (I talk about this in a bit more detail below)</li><li>If privacy isn't a huge concern, you can use any number of other providers, some of them quite affordable, and it's easy to switch so you're not locked into a single evil vendor</li><li>You can still use powerful LLMs without financially supporting deranged oligarchs who gamble with life on Earth so that they can always remain in power</li></ul><h2 id="chatgpt-and-claude-will-never-be-private">ChatGPT and Claude will <em>never</em> be private</h2><p>These companies only offer closed versions of their powerful models (despite the word "open" in "OpenAI"). You pay them for chat and/or API access, and they are allowed to look at everything you do.</p><p>They offer a checkbox so you can opt out of them using your data for training, but your chat sessions still aren't private. They have access to it all. They can look at it, share it with governments or companies, or do whatever they want. It's possible, likely even, that they won't use it for training if you've opted out, at least not right now. But do you really trust that long-term? Have they done anything to make anyone believe that they respect people's data?</p><h2 id="openai-and-anthropic-are-corporate-gatekeepers">OpenAI and Anthropic are corporate gatekeepers</h2><p>Their models have strict guardrails, as anyone who's tried to use them for anything cybersecurity-related has no doubt noticed.</p><p>Every session has a cop agent reading your transcript over your shoulder, making sure you're not using it for hacking. If it thinks you might be, the cop agent stops your session from continuing. It's very strict, and false positives happen all the time. Of course, not everyone has the cop watching them.</p><p>Some people get access to the models with looser restrictions or with none at all. OpenAI has <a href="https://openai.com/index/trusted-access-for-cyber/" rel="noreferrer">Trusted Access for Cyber</a> and Anthropic has the <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/cyber-verification-program" rel="noreferrer">Cyber Verification Program</a>. To gain access to more capable, less policed versions of their models, individuals and companies need to apply and be approved by OpenAI and Anthropic. A lot of US cybersecurity companies get access. Almost certainly US military contractors do too, and probably any parts of the US government that want it.</p><p>While there's some merit in restricting powerful models, handing the ultimate power to decide who gets access to them to Sam Altman and Dario Amodei – with consultation from the Trump Administration, of course – is a terrible, undemocratic idea.</p><p>I think a better approach is to simply not put yourself in a situation where you rely on ChatGPT or Claude. Instead of begging corporate overlords for privileged access, it's better to spend your time figuring out how to get models like GLM-5.3 to meet your needs. And it's not hard. These models are good, with the added benefit that they don't have a cop watching over your shoulder!</p><h2 id="open-weight-models-are-impressive">Open weight models are impressive</h2><p>So far, open weight models seem to be about four months behind the fancy proprietary models from OpenAI and Anthropic.</p><p>Four months is nothing.</p><p>I'm extremely impressed right now with GLM-5.3. There's plenty of data and benchmarks out there, but my vibes-based opinion from using it is that it's basically on par with Claude Opus 5.5 for software engineering.</p><p>I'm also impressed with GLM-5.3-Flash. This is similar to Claude Opus 4.8. It's much smaller and cheaper than GLM-5.3, and still incredibly capable. It's also actually feasible for an individual person to get hardware to run it, like if you can one day get your hands on a Mac Studio with 512GB of RAM.</p><p>GLM models are made by a Chinese company called Z.ai. While the models are impressive, <em>I don't recommend making an account directly with Z.ai</em> or sending them any of your data. Their coding harness ZCode (their version of Claude Code) was <a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/litigation/chinas-zai-disables-ai-coding-assistant-features-after-security-issue-2026-09-21/" rel="noreferrer">recently caught silently uploading</a> local source code repos and sending them back to Alibaba Cloud without consent. Also, it's safe to assume that Chinese companies will share any data you provide to them with the Chinese government.</p><p>Other open weight models from Chinese companies that are impressive include the DeepSeek, Qwen, and Kimi families of models. It's perfectly fine, safe, and secure to use these open weight models. Just don't trust the companies themselves with your chat sessions.</p><p>Another frontier AI lab worth a look is <a href="https://mistral.ai/" rel="noreferrer">Mistral</a>. As a French company, it's the only big player that's not Chinese or American. They just released an open weight frontier model called Mistral Large 4, aka <a href="https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4/" rel="noreferrer">Le Chonk</a>. I think it's quite a bit more reasonable to trust Mistral with your data compared to the Chinese or American companies.</p><h2 id="private-inference-with-tinfoil-or-maple-ai">Private inference with Tinfoil or Maple AI</h2><p>It's possible to have private AI, even without hosting it locally yourself, using secure enclaves. These aren't perfect, but I do think they provide a very meaningful difference in privacy compared to the status quo mode, which is "the provider can access everything with no restrictions."</p><p>The company that's doing the most work here is <a href="https://tinfoil.sh/" rel="noreferrer">Tinfoil</a>. If you want to learn the details, read their <a href="https://docs.tinfoil.sh/verification/secure-enclave-primer" rel="noreferrer">docs</a> on secure enclaves and remote attestation. Another company doing this is <a href="https://trymaple.ai/" rel="noreferrer">Maple AI</a> – although, technically, Maple uses Tinfoil for inference in the background.</p><p>Both Tinfoil and Maple are good options. Tinfoil is cheaper, but Maple is more consumer-focused.</p><p>If you want a ChatGPT-like experience, where you can talk to a chatbot, have it look stuff up for you on the internet, give it access to docs to read or do stuff with, access it in a mobile app, etc., then I'd go with Maple. There's a small free plan and a Pro plan that costs $20/month.</p><p>If you want to do real <a href="https://micahflee.com/agentic-coding-techniques/" rel="noreferrer">agentic coding</a> tasks like software engineering, reverse engineering, malware analysis, CTFs or pen tests, etc., then you should use Tinfoil instead. They charge by the token for private inference, so no monthly plan. GLM-5.3 currently costs $1.80 per 1M input tokens, $0.80 per 1M cached tokens, and $5.75 per 1M output tokens. You can use significantly fewer tokens if you choose the "low" reasoning level, and it's still very impressive.</p><h2 id="inference-without-the-privacy">Inference without the privacy</h2><p>If you don't want to pay a premium for private inference from Tinfoil or Maple, but you still want to free yourself from OpenAI and Anthropic, there are tons of less-private options that are also very cheap.</p><p>One that I've been playing with is <a href="https://opencode.ai/go" rel="noreferrer">OpenCode Go</a>. It costs $10/month for more than $10 worth of credits. This is an inference router, which basically means your API queries do something like this: your computer -&gt; OpenCode Go API -&gt; the actual inference provider. This means that both OpenCode Go and whatever backend provider they're using will have access to your session data. That said, the <a href="https://opencode.ai/docs/go/#privacy" rel="noreferrer">privacy section</a> of their docs promises which models data won't be used for training (most of them) and what the data retention is (zero days for most, including all the GLM, Qwen, Kimi, and DeepSeek models). Of course, it's just a promise. Nothing technical guarantees it.</p><p>There are plenty of other cheap options out there for non-private inference, if you just want to experiment with powerful open weight models.</p><h2 id="how-im-using-these-models">How I'm using these models</h2><p>I've been using both Tinfoil and OpenCode Go, configured with my current harness of choice, OpenCode. I've been mostly using GLM-5.3 on low reasoning, with great results. (Things change so quickly with the open weight models, who knows what will be best six months from now.)</p><p>I use OpenCode Go inference for completely public work, like when I'm implementing something that's already open source.</p><p>I use Tinfoil for everything else.</p><p>I've only just started working this way, but so far, I think the open weight models can easily completely replace my use of ChatGPT and Claude, at a comparable or cheaper price, with the added benefit that they're far more private and there are far fewer guardrails.</p>
    </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA['AI' Is Not a Tool by Rich Hickey (281 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/ai-tool-rich-hickey-kjl8e</link>
            <guid>50060269</guid>
            <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:00:25 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/ai-tool-rich-hickey-kjl8e">https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/ai-tool-rich-hickey-kjl8e</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50060269">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-test-id="article-content-blocks">
<!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>(I keep delivering this as a quip, here's a bit more)</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>First order of business - is '</span><span>tool</span><span>' going to have an interesting, distinguishing and thus illuminative meaning or be a mere platitudinous stand-in for "anything useful"?</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>We'll save tool as "anyone/thing that can be exploited" for the AI titans, for whom VCs, politicians, wannabe-titan-CEOs, software devs, rural towns etc are all 'tools'. The pockets on the front of my shirts are useful (I need to keep my reading glasses handy), but they are not tools. Similarly, fast food drive-thru windows are useful, but are not tools (more later).</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Some definitions of '</span><span>tool</span><span>':</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <ul>
        
    <li><span>a device or implement, especially one held in the hand, used to carry out a particular function</span><!----></li>
    <li><span>an instrument (such as a hammer or saw) used or worked by hand : an instrument used by a handicraftsperson or laborer in their work</span><!----></li>

      </ul>
  <!---->
        
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>What I like best - </span><span>A tool is something you </span><span>wield</span><span>.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <ul>
        
    <li><span>wield</span><span> - to use (as a tool or instrument) especially with full command or power : handle, manipulate, control</span><!----></li>

      </ul>
  <!---->
        
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Especially useful in that are:</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <ul>
        
    <li><span>Held in your hand - a source of both control and feedback, and an indication of the intimacy of the act of use</span><!----></li>
    <li><span>Power - force, energy transmission and direction, leverage etc</span><!----></li>
    <li><span>Control - intention and continuous directing of the course of events</span><!----></li>

      </ul>
  <!---->
        
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Tools are implements with which </span><span>you</span><span> do something, they do not do things for you. Tool use builds, rewards, and often requires, expertise and skill. The continuous application of technique and energy, and sensory feedback, are essential to the buildup of </span><span>expertise</span><span> (from experiri - "try").</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Good tools have many properties LLMs lack - they are predictable, reliable, deterministic, of bounded function, hopefully simple etc.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>What you learn while wielding a tool is something about the world, the task, the physics, logic etc of what you are trying to do or the situation you are trying to overcome. And often, something about yourself.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>As a lifelong tool builder and user, I know what tools are. LLMs are not tools.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Control, sensory feedback, intention, intimacy, continuous direction, expertise etc are all missing from LLM use. LLMs are not wielded. </span><span>LLMs are </span><span>automation</span><span>.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <div data-test-id="publishing-text-block">
        <ul>
        
    <li><span>automation</span><span> - (of a device or process) working by itself with little or no direct human control.</span><!----></li>

      </ul>
  <!---->
        
    </div>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>A drive-thru is not a tool for cooking, it is automation for avoiding cooking. LLMs are not tools with which you write code - they are automation with which you avoid writing code, to which you abdicate writing code. </span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>You don't direct the action of an LLM - you make requests and it acts on its own, without your energy or continuous direction. This request/automation cycle is not wielding, it's not control, it's not continuous, it's without feedback.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>What you are doing while using automation, instead of learning real-world truths, is attempting to divine the details du jour of some automaton. And striving to game them - e.g. ordering a custom burger (no onions) in order to get a freshly made one. Or filling </span><span>voodoo.md</span><span> with just the right incantations. You are only a software update from everything you've 'learned' being rendered obsolete by the latest algorithm. (A soggy cold burger from which the onions have been removed) You have traded working with the world to working for a capricious robot.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>It is a mistake to be haphazard about "tools" being equivalent to utility, e.g. saying - "the LLM didn't write my paper, I just used it as a tool to read and summarize some research papers". But you didn't </span><span>wield</span><span> it to read those papers (as you might a Kindle). It read the papers, not you. </span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>What do you miss by abdicating doing the reading? Pretty much everything. First and foremost, you miss learning how to write from those who can. You miss learning how to formulate an argument.  You miss noticing things. You miss loading your background mind with fodder for novel ideas. You miss building trust in some authors and suspicion of others. You miss all the jokes. You miss all of the edges. You miss learning how to think.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Tools don't get "misaligned", they don't "go rogue", they don't "escape containment", "conspire" etc.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>LLMs are not tools. I know many people use the phrase "just tools" simply to indicate "they are what you make of them". </span><span>Except that's not true</span><span>. Automation has intrinsic properties and differences from tools that you ignore at your peril.</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    <p>
          <span>Rich</span><!---->
        </p>
  
<!----><!---->                  

    
  
<!---->              </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
